      "thinking": "Opponent's at 5 with 8 lands available. Attacking with Warden and firebending gets me 9 mana for 3 pings, dropping them to 2 but not lethal—and I need to check if Warden survives a double block, since Parrot and Yue together deal 5 damage which kills it. Unagi's summoning sick but can still block, so opponent has three potential blockers: Parrot, Yue, and Unagi.\n\
\n\
If I don't attack, I can still activate twice off 8 lands for 3 damage, taking them to 3, then next turn attacking gets me the 9th mana for lethal—assuming Warden survives. But if I do attack this turn and they leave it unblocked, 2 combat damage plus 3 pings would be exactly lethal at 5 life; the risk is they'll just double-block to kill Warden and deny me the mana engine going forward. I also need to weigh their attack back—Yue, Parrot, and Unagi together could hit me for 10, dropping me from 11 to 1, so I'm considering blocking Unagi with Warden even though it dies.\n\
\n\
In the end, the safest line seems to be pinging twice now to drop them to 3 while keeping Warden home, absorbing their attack down to 1 life (or blocking Unagi and losing Warden, which I don't want), then finishing with attack plus three pings next turn for the win—accepting the risk that a pump spell or burn could kill me at 1 life in the meantime.\n\
\n\
